# C# Best Practices 🔷

Follow these when writing or reviewing any C# code.

## 1. Naming conventions 🏷️
- `PascalCase` for classes, methods, properties, constants, namespaces.
- `camelCase` for locals, parameters and private fields.
- Prefix private fields with `_` (e.g. `_userService`).
- Descriptive names; avoid ambiguous abbreviations.

## 2. Variables & types 📐
- Use `var` when the type is obvious; explicit types when they improve readability.
- Initialize variables at declaration when possible.
- Prefer `readonly` / `const` for values that never change:
```csharp
private readonly IUserService _userService;
private const int MaxRetries = 3;
```

## 3. Classes & encapsulation 🧩
- Small classes, single responsibility (SRP).
- Properties (not public fields) when logic/validation is needed.
- Prefer immutability: `record`, `init`-only properties.
```csharp
public record User(string Name, string Email);
```

## 4. Methods ⚙️
- Short, focused on one task; verb-based names (`GetUserById`, `ValidateInput`).
- Max 3-4 params; group into objects/records otherwise.
- Expression-bodied members (`=>`) for one-liners.

## 5. Error handling ❌
- `try-catch` only where the error can be handled meaningfully.
- Be specific with exception types; don't swallow generic `Exception`.
- Re-throw with `throw;` (never `throw ex;`) to preserve the stack trace.
- Validate params with `ArgumentException` / `ArgumentNullException`; custom exceptions when they add value.

## 6. Null safety 🕳️
- Use nullable reference types: `string?` for values that may be null.
- Prefer `?.` and `??`: `var name = user?.Name ?? "Unknown";`
- Avoid null where possible.

## 7. LINQ & collections 🔗
- Prefer LINQ over loops when it improves readability.
- Materialize (`ToList()`/`ToArray()`) only when needed; avoid multiple iterations over the same query.
- Use immutable collections when data won't change.

## 8. Async & await ⏳
- `async`/`await` for I/O; avoid `async void` (except event handlers).
- Accept a `CancellationToken` in async methods.
- `ConfigureAwait(false)` in libraries.
- Suffix async methods with `Async` (`GetUserByIdAsync`).

## 9. Dependency Injection 💉
- Inject via constructor; avoid `new` for testable dependencies.
- Register services in DI (here via `AddApplicationServices()` / `AddRepositories()`).
- Depend on interfaces, not concrete types.
- `Scoped` for `DbContext`, `Transient` for stateless services, `Singleton` with care.

## 10. Comments & docs 📝
- XML comments (`///`) on public APIs and complex methods; document params, returns, thrown exceptions.
- No comments that state the obvious; code should be self-explanatory.
- `TODO`/`FIXME` with clear context.

## 11. Security 🔒
- Never hardcode secrets/keys; use User Secrets, env vars or Key Vault.
- Validate and sanitize all user input.
- Parameterized SQL queries; least-privilege permissions.

## 12. Testing 🧪
- Unit test critical business logic; mock dependencies (no real I/O).
- Names: `MethodName_Scenario_ExpectedBehavior`.
- Structure with AAA (Arrange, Act, Assert).

## 13. Performance ⚡
- Avoid unnecessary boxing; use generics.
- `StringBuilder` for string concatenation in loops.
- Consider `Span<T>`/`Memory<T>` for buffers; object pooling for hot allocations.

## 14. Structure & organization 📂
- One class per file; file name matches class name.
- Namespaces mirror folder structure.
- Separate concerns: Controllers, Services, Repositories, Models, DTOs.
- DTO = data passed between layers; Entities = DB tables. Keep layers in separate projects.

## 15. Readability & formatting ✨
- Use regions sparingly; prefer smaller files.
- Group related members; consistent formatting (`.editorconfig`).
- Keep files small (~200-300 lines) for easy navigation.
